            <div id="frameworksCatalogContainer">
                <details class="framework-group-card" data-fw-group="ANSSI-BP-028" open>
                    <summary class="framework-group-header">
                        <span class="group-header-left">
                            <span class="group-chevron">&#9654;</span>
                            <span class="group-title">ANSSI — Recommandations de sécurité relatives à un système GNU/Linux (BP-028)</span>
                            <span class="framework-level-pill">v2.0 (ANSSI-BP-028)</span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="triage-row-badges">
                            <span class="mini-sev badge-high">4/7 Triggered (4 findings)</span>
                        </span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="framework-group-meta">
                        <p>French National Cybersecurity Agency (ANSSI) baseline security guide for GNU/Linux systems. Section 6.3.1 &#34;Unix traditional model&#34; (v2.0 R38–R44; v1.2 R57–R64 per the backward compatibility table in Appendix C.3) defines controls for sudo: a dedicated execution group, secure Defaults directives, non-root target users, strict limitation of commands requiring the EXEC directive, a ban on negations, strict argument specifications, and safe file editing via sudoedit.</p>
                        <div class="framework-meta-links">
                            <span><strong>Authority:</strong> Agence nationale de la sécurité des systèmes d&#39;information (ANSSI)</span>
                            <span><strong>Official Publication:</strong> <a href="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux</a></span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="framework-reqs-body">
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-anssi-r38" data-fw-id="ANSSI-BP-028" data-req-triggered="true">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">ANSSI-BP-028 R38</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Minimal / Intermediate / Enhanced (R38)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Creating a Group Dedicated to the Use of sudo</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-med">1 Violation (MEDIUM)</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux">R38 - Creating a group dedicated to the use of sudo: A group dedicated to the use of sudo must be created. Only the members of this group should have the right to run sudo. Example: # ls -al /usr/bin/sudo → -rwsr-x---. 2 root sudogrp [...] /usr/bin/sudo. Warning: this modification can be overwritten by an update of the sudo package; it is recommended to check the access rights of the file after an update and apply them again if needed.</blockquote>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote-fr" lang="fr" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux"><strong>Traduction française (ANSSI BP-028) :</strong> R38 - Créer un groupe dédié à l&#39;utilisation de sudo : Un groupe dédié à l&#39;utilisation de sudo doit être créé. Seuls les membres de ce groupe doivent avoir le droit d&#39;exécuter sudo. Exemple : # ls -al /usr/bin/sudo → -rwsr-x---. 2 root sudogrp [...] /usr/bin/sudo.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> sudo is a setuid root executable installed by default with open execution rights. Restricting execution to a dedicated group reduces the attack surface when the system or the binary itself is affected by exploitable vulnerabilities (e.g. CVE-2019-14287, CVE-2021-3156). A sudo binary that is writable by group or other, or not owned by root, defeats this restriction and allows immediate local root compromise.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>sudo chown root:root /usr/bin/sudo
sudo chmod 4755 /usr/bin/sudo
# Restrict execution to a dedicated group (example):
sudo groupadd sudogrp
sudo chown root:sudogrp /usr/bin/sudo
sudo chmod 4750 /usr/bin/sudo</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-SYS-PERM-008" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-SYS-PERM-008">SUDO-SYS-PERM-008</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-SYS-PERM-009" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-SYS-PERM-009">SUDO-SYS-PERM-009</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-SYS-PERM-010" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-SYS-PERM-010">SUDO-SYS-PERM-010</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-req-code="ANSSI-BP-028 R38" data-req-rules="SUDO-SYS-PERM-008,SUDO-SYS-PERM-009,SUDO-SYS-PERM-010" data-req-comps="ANSSI-BP-028 R38">View Triggered Findings (1)</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-filesystem-perms">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-anssi-r39" data-fw-id="ANSSI-BP-028" data-req-triggered="true">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">ANSSI-BP-028 R39</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Minimal / Intermediate (R39)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Sudo Configuration Guidelines</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-high">3 Violations (HIGH)</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux">R39 - Sudo configuration guidelines: The following guidelines must be enabled by default: noexec (applies the NOEXEC tag by default on the commands); requiretty (requires the user to have a tty login); use_pty (uses a pseudo-tty when a command is executed); umask=0077 (forces umask to a more restrictive mask); ignore_dot (ignores the &#34;.&#34; in $PATH); env_reset (resets the environment variables). Guidelines for the /etc/sudoers configuration file: Defaults noexec,requiretty,use_pty,umask=0077 / Defaults ignore_dot,env_reset. It is then possible to override a default value if needed when an entry is added for a user or for a group (e.g. myuser ALL = EXEC: /usr/bin/mycommand).</blockquote>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote-fr" lang="fr" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux"><strong>Traduction française (ANSSI BP-028) :</strong> R39 - Recommandations de configuration pour sudo : Les directives suivantes doivent être activées par défaut : noexec (applique par défaut la balise NOEXEC aux commandes) ; requiretty (exige que l&#39;utilisateur dispose d&#39;un tty) ; use_pty (utilise un pseudo-tty lors de l&#39;exécution d&#39;une commande) ; umask=0077 (force un umask plus restrictif) ; ignore_dot (ignore le « . » dans $PATH) ; env_reset (réinitialise les variables d&#39;environnement). Dans le fichier /etc/sudoers : Defaults noexec,requiretty,use_pty,umask=0077 puis Defaults ignore_dot,env_reset.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Without env_reset and ignore_dot, callers can hijack the execution environment through inherited variables or relative PATH entries. Without use_pty and requiretty, child processes can snoop or inject keystrokes into the calling terminal. Without noexec, allowed utilities can spawn interactive shells. Without umask=0077, files created by root inherit the caller&#39;s permissive umask.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Defaults noexec,requiretty,use_pty,umask=0077
Defaults ignore_dot,env_reset</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-003" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-003">SUDO-DEF-003</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-007" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-007">SUDO-DEF-007</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-008" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-008">SUDO-DEF-008</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-009" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-009">SUDO-DEF-009</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-010" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-010">SUDO-DEF-010</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-011" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-011">SUDO-DEF-011</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-GTFO-003" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-GTFO-003">SUDO-GTFO-003</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-req-code="ANSSI-BP-028 R39" data-req-rules="SUDO-DEF-003,SUDO-DEF-007,SUDO-DEF-008,SUDO-DEF-009,SUDO-DEF-010,SUDO-DEF-011,SUDO-GTFO-003" data-req-comps="ANSSI-BP-028 R39">View Triggered Findings (3)</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-anssi-r40" data-fw-id="ANSSI-BP-028" data-req-triggered="true">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">ANSSI-BP-028 R40</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Minimal / Intermediate (R40)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Using Unprivileged Users as Target for sudo Commands</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-crit">1 Violation (CRITICAL)</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux">R40 - Using unprivileged users as target for sudo commands: The targeted users of a rule should be, as much as possible, non privileged users (i.e.: non root). It is common that the command to be executed does not require an elevated privilege level (editing a file whose owner is not root, sending a signal to an unprivileged process, ...). In order to limit any attempt of privilege escalation through a command, it is better to apply regular user rights.</blockquote>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote-fr" lang="fr" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux"><strong>Traduction française (ANSSI BP-028) :</strong> R40 - Utiliser des utilisateurs non privilégiés comme cible des commandes sudo : Les utilisateurs ciblés par une règle doivent être, autant que possible, des utilisateurs non privilégiés (c&#39;est-à-dire non root). Il est fréquent que la commande à exécuter ne nécessite pas un niveau de privilège élevé ; afin de limiter toute tentative d&#39;élévation de privilèges via une commande, il est préférable d&#39;appliquer les droits de l&#39;utilisateur régulier.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Running delegated commands as root when the task does not require it turns any argument-injection or binary flaw into a direct root compromise. Targeting a dedicated unprivileged service account contains the blast radius of a misused rule.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Runas_Alias WEB_SVC_USER = www-data
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh &#34;&#34;</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-req-code="ANSSI-BP-028 R40" data-req-rules="" data-req-comps="ANSSI-BP-028 R40">View Triggered Findings (1)</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-anssi-r41" data-fw-id="ANSSI-BP-028" data-req-triggered="true">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">ANSSI-BP-028 R41</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Minimal / Intermediate / Enhanced (R41)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Limiting the Number of Commands Requiring the Use of the EXEC Directive</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-crit">1 Violation (CRITICAL)</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux">R41 - Limiting the number of commands requiring the use of the EXEC directive: The commands requiring the execution of sub-processes, EXEC directive must be explicitly listed and their use should be reduced to a strict minimum. Functionally rich commands can be executed via sudo, like text editors (vi). They may allow execution of sub-commands in the environment created by sudo and thus enable a user to run other software with privileges not foreseen initially.</blockquote>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote-fr" lang="fr" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux"><strong>Traduction française (ANSSI BP-028) :</strong> R41 - Limiter le nombre de commandes nécessitant l&#39;utilisation de la directive EXEC : Les commandes nécessitant l&#39;exécution de sous-processus (directive EXEC) doivent être explicitement listées et leur usage doit être réduit au strict minimum. Des commandes fonctionnellement riches, comme les éditeurs de texte (vi), peuvent permettre l&#39;exécution de sous-commandes dans l&#39;environnement créé par sudo.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> The noexec default (R39) blocks child-process execution, but sudo can override the functions allowing execution of other software when a command genuinely requires sub-processes. Every EXEC-requiring command (editors, pagers, GTFObins binaries with shell or command capabilities) must be an explicit, minimal exception; otherwise users can spawn unanticipated privileged processes.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code># Keep EXEC-requiring commands an explicit, minimal exception list:
Cmnd_Alias EXEC_CMDS = /usr/bin/systemctl status app
# Replace GTFObins-capable binaries with fixed-argument root-owned wrapper scripts:
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh &#34;&#34;</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-GTFO-001" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-GTFO-001">SUDO-GTFO-001</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-GTFO-002" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-GTFO-002">SUDO-GTFO-002</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-req-code="ANSSI-BP-028 R41" data-req-rules="SUDO-GTFO-001,SUDO-GTFO-002" data-req-comps="ANSSI-BP-028 R41">View Triggered Findings (1)</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-wrapper-script">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-anssi-r42" data-fw-id="ANSSI-BP-028" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">ANSSI-BP-028 R42</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Minimal / Intermediate (R42)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Banishing the Negations in sudo Policies</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux">R42 - Banishing the negations in sudo policies: Policies applied by sudo through the sudoers file should not involve any negation. Specifying access right using negation, blacklist approach, is inefficient and can be easily circumvented. Example (to avoid absolutely, this rule can be easily circumvented): user ALL = ALL,!/bin/sh - just copy the binary /bin/sh to a different name to make it executable again through the rule directive ALL.</blockquote>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote-fr" lang="fr" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux"><strong>Traduction française (ANSSI BP-028) :</strong> R42 - Proscrire les négations dans les politiques sudo : Les politiques appliquées par sudo via le fichier sudoers ne doivent comporter aucune négation. La spécification de droits par négation (approche par liste noire) est inefficace et peut être contournée facilement. Exemple (à proscrire absolument) : user ALL = ALL,!/bin/sh - il suffit de copier le binaire /bin/sh sous un autre nom pour pouvoir l&#39;exécuter via la règle ALL.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Exclusion-based security controls using &#39;!&#39; are brittle: negated commands (e.g. &#39;ALL, !/bin/sh&#39;) are trivially bypassed by copying or linking binaries, and negated Runas or Host specifications have led to critical authorization bypasses (such as CVE-2019-14287 with UID -1 and CVE-2025-32462).</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code># Define explicit positive aliases (zero &#39;!&#39; exclusions):
User_Alias  WEB_OPERATORS = %sudo_webops
Runas_Alias WEB_SVC_USER  = www-data
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh &#34;&#34;</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-001" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-001">SUDO-CMD-001</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-008" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-008">SUDO-CMD-008</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-anssi-r43" data-fw-id="ANSSI-BP-028" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">ANSSI-BP-028 R43</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Minimal / Intermediate (R43)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Defining the Arguments in sudo Specifications</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux">R43 - Defining the arguments in sudo specifications: All commands in the sudoers configuration file must strictly specify the arguments allowed to be used for a given user. The use of * (wildcard) in the rules should be avoided as much as possible. The absence of arguments in a command must be specified by the presence of an empty chain (&#34;&#34;). Example: user ALL = dmesg &#34;&#34; - restrict the arguments in order to prevent the user from reading any arbitrary file through the --file option.</blockquote>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote-fr" lang="fr" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux"><strong>Traduction française (ANSSI BP-028) :</strong> R43 - Définir les arguments dans les spécifications sudo : Toutes les commandes du fichier de configuration sudoers doivent préciser strictement les arguments autorisés pour un utilisateur donné. L&#39;utilisation du joker * dans les règles doit être évitée autant que possible. L&#39;absence d&#39;arguments dans une commande doit être spécifiée par la présence d&#39;une chaîne vide (&#34;&#34;). Exemple : user ALL = dmesg &#34;&#34;.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Any argument can modify quite significantly the behavior of a program, whether regarding the realized operation (read, write, delete, ...) or accessed resources (path in a file system tree). When a command in sudoers has no argument specification, sudo permits arbitrary caller-supplied flags; wildcards (*) additionally match arbitrary flags and path traversal sequences.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code># Zero arguments allowed (explicit &#34;&#34;):
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh &#34;&#34;
# Fixed argument allowlist:
SECURITY_AUDIT ALL = (root) PASSWD: NOEXEC: /usr/sbin/auditctl -s</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-002" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-002">SUDO-CMD-002</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-003" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-003">SUDO-CMD-003</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-004" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-004">SUDO-CMD-004</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-006" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-006">SUDO-CMD-006</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-anssi-r44" data-fw-id="ANSSI-BP-028" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">ANSSI-BP-028 R44</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Minimal / Intermediate (R44)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Editing Files Securely with sudo</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux">R44 - Editing files securely with sudo: A file requiring sudo to be edited must be edited through the sudoedit command. The sudoedit text editor solves these problems by editing a temporary version of the file with the current user rights, then replacing the original file by this temporary version once the operation is finished. The editor runs thus only with the current user rights and never with the privileges of the target user.</blockquote>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote-fr" lang="fr" cite="https://cyber.gouv.fr/publications/recommandations-de-securite-relatives-un-systeme-gnulinux"><strong>Traduction française (ANSSI BP-028) :</strong> R44 - Éditer des fichiers de manière sécurisée avec sudo : Un fichier nécessitant sudo pour être modifié doit être édité via la commande sudoedit. L&#39;éditeur travaille sur une version temporaire du fichier avec les droits de l&#39;utilisateur courant, puis remplace le fichier original une fois l&#39;opération terminée. L&#39;éditeur ne s&#39;exécute qu&#39;avec les droits de l&#39;utilisateur courant, jamais avec les privilèges de l&#39;utilisateur cible.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> A text editor is a functionally rich program that can open and edit other files via internal commands or even run a shell, offering a full privileged environment to a user. sudoedit runs the editor under the unprivileged caller UID on a temporary copy and swaps it back only on exit.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: sudoedit /etc/nginx/conf.d/app-vhost.conf</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-007" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-007">SUDO-CMD-007</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                    </div>
                </details>
                <details class="framework-group-card" data-fw-group="CIS-BENCHMARK" open>
                    <summary class="framework-group-header">
                        <span class="group-header-left">
                            <span class="group-chevron">&#9654;</span>
                            <span class="group-title">CIS Linux Benchmarks — Section 5.2 Configure Privilege Escalation (RHEL / Ubuntu / Debian)</span>
                            <span class="framework-level-pill">RHEL 9 v2.0.0 / Ubuntu 24.04 LTS v1.0.0 numbering (Controls 5.2.1 – 5.2.7)</span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="triage-row-badges">
                            <span class="mini-sev badge-high">2/7 Triggered (3 findings)</span>
                        </span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="framework-group-meta">
                        <p>Consensus-driven configuration standards for hardening Linux privilege escalation. Section 5.2 of the current CIS Linux benchmarks (identical numbering in RHEL 9 v2.0.0 and Ubuntu 24.04 LTS v1.0.0; older releases numbered these controls 5.3.x) requires sudo to be installed, commands to run inside a pseudo-terminal, a dedicated sudo log file, a password for escalation without NOPASSWD, re-authentication not disabled via !authenticate, and a credential cache timeout of at most 15 minutes, and restricts access to the su command.</p>
                        <div class="framework-meta-links">
                            <span><strong>Authority:</strong> Center for Internet Security (CIS)</span>
                            <span><strong>Official Publication:</strong> <a href="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks</a></span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="framework-reqs-body">
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-cis-5-2-1" data-fw-id="CIS-BENCHMARK" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">CIS-5.2.1</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Level 1 — Server &amp; Workstation</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Ensure sudo is installed</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks">CIS 5.2.1 - Ensure sudo is installed: &#39;sudo allows a permitted user to execute a command as the superuser or another user, as specified by the security policy.&#39; Audit: &#39;Verify that sudo is installed. Run the following command: # dnf list sudo&#39;. Note: sudo-check verifies sudo availability on the target host in system mode via &#39;sudo --version&#39;; it does not audit package installation.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> The default security policy is sudoers, which is configured via the file /etc/sudoers and any entries in /etc/sudoers.d. Without the sudo package installed, privilege escalation policy cannot be enforced or audited.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code># dnf install sudo   # RHEL / SUSE
# apt install sudo    # Debian / Ubuntu</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-cis-5-2-2" data-fw-id="CIS-BENCHMARK" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">CIS-5.2.2</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Level 1 — Server &amp; Workstation</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Ensure sudo commands use pty</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks">CIS 5.2.2 - Ensure sudo commands use pty: &#39;sudo can be configured to run only from a pseudo terminal (pseudo-pty).&#39; Rationale: &#39;Attackers can run a malicious program using sudo which would fork a background process that remains even when the main program has finished executing.&#39; Remediation: &#39;Edit the file /etc/sudoers with visudo or a file in /etc/sudoers.d/ with visudo -f &lt;PATH TO FILE&gt; and add the following line: Defaults use_pty&#39;.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Running sudo commands inside a dedicated pseudo-terminal (PTY) isolates the privileged process I/O streams and prevents backgrounded subprocesses from snooping or injecting keystrokes into the invoking user&#39;s controlling terminal.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Defaults use_pty</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-003" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-003">SUDO-DEF-003</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-cis-5-2-3" data-fw-id="CIS-BENCHMARK" data-req-triggered="true">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">CIS-5.2.3</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Level 1 — Server &amp; Workstation</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Ensure sudo log file exists</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-crit">2 Violations (CRITICAL)</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks">CIS 5.2.3 - Ensure sudo log file exists: &#39;The Defaults logfile entry sets the path to the sudo log file. Setting a path turns on logging to a file; negating this option turns it off. By default, sudo logs via syslog.&#39; Rationale: &#39;Defining a dedicated log file for sudo simplifies auditing of sudo commands and creation of auditd rules for sudo.&#39; Remediation example: &#39;Defaults logfile=&#34;/var/log/sudo.log&#34;&#39;. Note: log_input and log_output are defense-in-depth additions; they are not part of this CIS control.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> A dedicated sudo log file simplifies auditing of privileged commands and the creation of auditd rules for sudo; without it, sudo activity is only available through syslog.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Defaults logfile=&#34;/var/log/sudo.log&#34;</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-req-code="CIS-5.2.3" data-req-rules="" data-req-comps="CIS-5.2.3">View Triggered Findings (2)</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-cis-5-2-4" data-fw-id="CIS-BENCHMARK" data-req-triggered="true">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">CIS-5.2.4</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Level 2 — Server &amp; Workstation</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Ensure users must provide password for escalation (no NOPASSWD)</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-high">1 Violation (HIGH)</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks">CIS 5.2.4 - Ensure users must provide password for escalation: &#39;The operating system must be configured so that users must provide a password for privilege escalation.&#39; Audit: &#39;Verify the operating system requires users to supply a password for privilege escalation. Check the configuration of the /etc/sudoers and /etc/sudoers.d/* files with the following command: # grep -r &#34;^[^#].*NOPASSWD&#34; /etc/sudoers*&#39;. Remediation: &#39;Remove any line with occurrences of NOPASSWD tags in the file.&#39;</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Without re-authentication, users may access resources or perform tasks for which they do not have authorization. When operating systems provide the capability to escalate a functional capability, it is critical the user re-authenticate.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Defaults authenticate
# Always require PASSWD on User_Spec rules:
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh &#34;&#34;</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-003" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-003">SUDO-CMD-003</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-005" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-005">SUDO-CMD-005</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-req-code="CIS-5.2.4" data-req-rules="SUDO-CMD-003,SUDO-CMD-005" data-req-comps="CIS-5.2.4">View Triggered Findings (1)</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-cis-5-2-5" data-fw-id="CIS-BENCHMARK" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">CIS-5.2.5</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Level 1 — Server &amp; Workstation</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Ensure re-authentication for privilege escalation is not disabled globally (no !authenticate)</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks">CIS 5.2.5 - Ensure re-authentication for privilege escalation is not disabled globally: &#39;The operating system must be configured so that users must re-authenticate for privilege escalation.&#39; Audit: &#39;Verify the operating system requires users to re-authenticate for privilege escalation. Check the configuration of the /etc/sudoers and /etc/sudoers.d/* files with the following command: # grep -r &#34;^[^#].*\!authenticate&#34; /etc/sudoers*&#39;. Remediation: &#39;Remove any occurrences of !authenticate tags in the file(s).&#39;</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Without re-authentication, users may access resources or perform tasks for which they do not have authorization. When operating systems provide the capability to escalate a functional capability, it is critical the user re-authenticate.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Defaults authenticate</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-012" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-012">SUDO-DEF-012</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-cis-5-2-6" data-fw-id="CIS-BENCHMARK" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">CIS-5.2.6</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Level 1 — Server &amp; Workstation</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Ensure sudo authentication timeout is configured correctly (&lt;= 15 minutes)</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks">CIS 5.2.6 - Ensure sudo authentication timeout is configured correctly: &#39;sudo caches used credentials for a default of 5 minutes. This is for ease of use when there are multiple administrative tasks to perform. The timeout can be modified to suit local security policies.&#39; Audit: &#39;Ensure that the caching timeout is no more than 15 minutes. # grep -roP &#34;timestamp_timeout=\K[0-9]*&#34; /etc/sudoers* Note: A value of -1 means that the timeout is disabled.&#39; Remediation: &#39;If the currently configured timeout is larger than 15 minutes, edit the file listed in the audit section with visudo -f &lt;PATH TO FILE&gt; and modify the entry timestamp_timeout= to 15 minutes or less as per your site policy.&#39;</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Setting a timeout value reduces the window of opportunity for unauthorized privileged access to another user.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Defaults timestamp_timeout=15</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-015" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-015">SUDO-DEF-015</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-cis-5-2-7" data-fw-id="CIS-BENCHMARK" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">CIS-5.2.7</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Level 1 — Server &amp; Workstation</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Ensure access to the su command is restricted</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://www.cisecurity.org/cis-benchmarks">CIS 5.2.7 - Ensure access to the su command is restricted: &#39;The su command allows a user to run a command or shell as another user. The program has been superseded by sudo, which allows for more granular control over privileged access.&#39; Remediation: &#39;Create an empty group that will be specified for use of the su command. Example: # groupadd sugroup. Add the following line to the /etc/pam.d/su file, specifying the empty group: auth required pam_wheel.so use_uid group=sugroup&#39;. Note: sudo-check audits sudoers policies only; it does not inspect /etc/pam.d/su.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Restricting the use of su, and using sudo in its place, provides system administrators better control of the escalation of user privileges to execute privileged commands, as well as a better logging and audit mechanism.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code># groupadd sugroup
echo &#39;auth required pam_wheel.so use_uid group=sugroup&#39; &gt;&gt; /etc/pam.d/su</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                    </div>
                </details>
                <details class="framework-group-card" data-fw-group="DISA-STIG" open>
                    <summary class="framework-group-header">
                        <span class="group-header-left">
                            <span class="group-chevron">&#9654;</span>
                            <span class="group-title">DISA Security Technical Implementation Guide (RHEL / Ubuntu / General UNIX STIG)</span>
                            <span class="framework-level-pill">RHEL 8/9 STIG &amp; Ubuntu 22.04/24.04 STIG</span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="triage-row-badges">
                            <span class="mini-sev badge-compliant">2 Requirements — 0 Violations</span>
                        </span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="framework-group-meta">
                        <p>U.S. Department of Defense (DoD) mandatory configuration requirements for operating systems. Requires a password and re-authentication for privilege escalation, prohibits NOPASSWD and !authenticate, requires the invoking user&#39;s password for sudo (prohibiting rootpw, targetpw, and runaspw), requires a non-negative sudo timestamp timeout, and requires administrator-grade sudo rules to elevate the SELinux context. The RHEL 9 and Ubuntu LTS STIGs carry equivalent findings.</p>
                        <div class="framework-meta-links">
                            <span><strong>Authority:</strong> Defense Information Systems Agency (DISA)</span>
                            <span><strong>Official Publication:</strong> <a href="https://public.cyber.mil/stigs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://public.cyber.mil/stigs/</a></span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="framework-reqs-body">
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-stig-reauth" data-fw-id="DISA-STIG" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">DISA-STIG</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">CAT II (Medium)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Mandatory Password Re-Authentication for Privilege Escalation (NOPASSWD / !authenticate Prohibited, Non-Negative Credential Cache Timeout)</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://public.cyber.mil/stigs/">RHEL 8 STIG, SV-230271 (RHEL-08-010380): &#39;RHEL 8 must require users to provide a password for privilege escalation.&#39; SV-230272 (RHEL-08-010381): &#39;RHEL 8 must require users to reauthenticate for privilege escalation.&#39; SV-237643 (RHEL-08-010383): &#39;RHEL 8 must require re-authentication when using the &#34;sudo&#34; command.&#39; Fix text: &#39;Configure the &#34;sudo&#34; command to require re-authentication. Edit the /etc/sudoers file: Add or modify the following line: Defaults timestamp_timeout=[value] Note: The &#34;[value]&#34; must be a number that is greater than or equal to &#34;0&#34;.&#39; Equivalent findings: RHEL 9 STIG V-258084 and RHEL-09-611085; Ubuntu 24.04 LTS STIG V-270707.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Without mandatory password verification and a bounded credential cache, any compromise of an unprivileged process allows silent, unauthenticated execution of administrative commands across sessions.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>Defaults authenticate
Defaults timestamp_timeout=5</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-012" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-012">SUDO-DEF-012</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-015" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-015">SUDO-DEF-015</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-003" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-003">SUDO-CMD-003</button>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-005" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-005">SUDO-CMD-005</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-stig-selinux-context" data-fw-id="DISA-STIG" data-req-triggered="false">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">DISA-STIG</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">CAT II (Medium)</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">SELinux Context Elevation for sudo Administrators (TYPE=sysadm_t ROLE=sysadm_r)</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-compliant">0 Violations — Compliant</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://public.cyber.mil/stigs/">RHEL 8 STIG, SV-272484 (RHEL-08-010455): &#39;RHEL 8 must elevate the SELinux context when an administrator calls the sudo command.&#39; Check: &#39;If a designated sudoers administrator group or account(s) is not configured to elevate the SELinux type and role to &#34;sysadm_t&#34; and &#34;sysadm_r&#34; with the use of the sudo command, this is a finding.&#39; Fix: &#39;Use the following example to build the &lt;customfile&gt; in the /etc/sudoers.d directory to allow any administrator belonging to a designated sudoers admin group to elevate their SELinux context with the use of the sudo command: %{designated_group_or_user_name} ALL=(ALL) TYPE=sysadm_t ROLE=sysadm_r ALL&#39;</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Without an explicit SELinux type and role transition, commands run via sudo execute in the invoking user&#39;s SELinux context, so privileged administration either fails under the policy or forces the login domain to be granted administrator privileges, eroding the separation between user space and admin space.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code>%sudo_admins ALL=(ALL) TYPE=sysadm_t ROLE=sysadm_r ALL</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
                                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Mapped Audit Rules:</strong>
                                    <button type="button" class="compliance-badge" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-013" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-013">SUDO-CMD-013</button>
                                </div>
                                <div class="req-action-links">
                                    <button type="button" class="jump-btn" data-jump-tpl="tpl-sudoers-baseline">View in Hardened Template</button>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </article>
                    </div>
                </details>
                <details class="framework-group-card" data-fw-group="MITRE-ATTACK" open>
                    <summary class="framework-group-header">
                        <span class="group-header-left">
                            <span class="group-chevron">&#9654;</span>
                            <span class="group-title">MITRE ATT&amp;CK Enterprise Matrix — Privilege Escalation &amp; Defense Evasion</span>
                            <span class="framework-level-pill">ATT&amp;CK v16 — Technique T1548.003</span>
                        </span>
                        <span class="triage-row-badges">
                            <span class="mini-sev badge-high">1/1 Triggered (2 findings)</span>
                        </span>
                    </summary>
                    <div class="framework-group-meta">
                        <p>Adversary tactics and techniques for abusing sudo policies, credential caching, and GTFObins living-off-the-land binaries (shell escapes, command execution, arbitrary file read/write, library loading, and SUID/sudo preservation).</p>
                        <div class="framework-meta-links">
                            <span><strong>Authority:</strong> MITRE Corporation &amp; GTFObins Project</span>
                            <span><strong>Official Publication:</strong> <a href="https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/003/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/003/</a></span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="framework-reqs-body">
                        <article class="framework-req-card" id="req-mitre-t1548-003" data-fw-id="MITRE-ATTACK" data-req-triggered="true">
                            <div class="framework-req-header">
                                <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                                    <div class="finding-meta-top">
                                        <span class="framework-req-code">MITRE-T1548.003</span>
                                        <span class="framework-level-pill">Privilege Escalation / Defense Evasion</span>
                                    </div>
                                    <h3 class="finding-title">Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Sudo and Sudo Caching (GTFObins Escalation Vectors)</h3>
                                </div>
                                <div class="finding-header-right">
                                    <span class="sev-badge badge-crit">2 Violations (CRITICAL)</span>
                                </div>
                            </div>

                            <div class="verbatim-box">
                                <span class="verbatim-label">Verbatim Standard Requirement</span>
                                <blockquote class="verbatim-quote" cite="https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1548/003/">MITRE ATT&amp;CK T1548.003: Adversaries may perform sudo caching and/or use the sudoers file to elevate privileges. Adversaries may abuse poor sudoers configurations (such as NOPASSWD, wildcards, missing argument restrictions, or GTFObins-capable binaries with shell, command, file-read, file-write, or library-load capabilities) to spawn elevated shells or modify system files.

Mitigations (M1026 Privileged Account Management, M1028 Operating System Configuration, M1038 Execution Prevention): Restrict sudoers rules to explicit fixed-argument commands, enforce NOEXEC to block child process execution, and replace direct access to interpreters/editors/pagers/archivers with root-owned wrapper scripts pinned by SHA-256 digest.</blockquote>
                            </div>

                            <p class="finding-desc"><strong>Security Rationale:</strong> Hundreds of standard Unix utilities (find, vim, less, awk, python, tar, journalctl, systemctl, etc.) include legitimate features to execute subshells, load shared libraries, or read/write arbitrary files. Note that NOEXEC only mitigates child process execution (&#39;shell&#39; and &#39;command&#39; functions) and does NOT prevent &#39;file-read&#39;, &#39;file-write&#39;, or &#39;library-load&#39; GTFObins vectors.</p>

                            <div class="finding-remediation">
                                <div class="remediation-code-header">
                                    <span class="remediation-code-label">Required Sudoers Directives &amp; Controls</span>
                                    <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                                </div>
                                <pre tabindex="0"><code># 1. Enforce global NOEXEC and PTY isolation:
Defaults noexec, use_pty, requiretty

# 2. Replace GTFObins binaries with fixed-argument root-owned wrapper scripts pinned by SHA-256:
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: \
    sha256:e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855 \
    /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh &#34;&#34;</code></pre>
                            </div>

                            <div class="req-cross-links">
