            </div>
            <article class="template-section-card" id="tpl-sudoers-baseline">
                <div class="template-section-header">
                    <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                        <div class="finding-meta-top">
                            <span class="framework-req-code">Primary Policy</span>
                            <span class="location-pill">/etc/sudoers (mode 0440, owner root:root)</span>
                        </div>
                        <h3 class="finding-title">1. Complete Hardened /etc/sudoers Baseline Configuration</h3>
                    </div>
                    <button type="button" class="action-btn" data-action="copy-section-pre">Copy Template</button>
                </div>
                <p class="finding-desc">Drop-in replacement baseline for /etc/sudoers implementing all ANSSI BP-028 v2.0 (R38–R44), CIS Benchmark (5.2.1–5.2.7), DISA STIG, and MITRE ATT&amp;CK controls. Verified to produce zero findings under sudo-check.</p>
                <ul class="template-highlights">
                    <li>Enforces Defaults env_reset, !setenv, ignore_dot, and a strict root-owned secure_path.</li>
                    <li>Isolates terminal I/O via use_pty and requiretty, blocks child shell escapes via noexec, and enforces umask=0077.</li>
                    <li>Requires password authentication (authenticate, !visiblepw, !pwfeedback) with passwd_timeout=1, timestamp_timeout=5, and timestamp_type=tty.</li>
                    <li>Hardens sudoedit with !sudoedit_follow and sudoedit_checkdir and enables full input/output audit logging.</li>
                    <li>Uses explicit positive aliases (zero &#39;!&#39; negations), non-root Runas service accounts, explicit argument pinning (&#34;&#34;), and SHA-256 digests.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="finding-remediation">
                    <div class="remediation-code-header">
                        <span class="remediation-code-label">/etc/sudoers (mode 0440, owner root:root)</span>
                        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                    </div>
                    <pre tabindex="0"><code># ==============================================================================
# Hardened /etc/sudoers Reference Baseline Policy
# Standards: ANSSI BP-028 v2.0 (R38-R44), CIS Benchmark 5.2.1-5.2.7, DISA STIG
# Validation: visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers &amp;&amp; sudo-check scan /etc/sudoers
# ==============================================================================

# --- 1. Environment Hardening (ANSSI BP-028 R39: env_reset, ignore_dot) ---
Defaults env_reset
Defaults !setenv
Defaults ignore_dot
Defaults secure_path=&#34;/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin&#34;
Defaults env_keep=&#34;LANG LC_ALL LC_CTYPE LC_MESSAGES TERM COLORTERM&#34;

# --- 2. Terminal Isolation, Subprocess Control &amp; Umask (ANSSI BP-028 R39 / CIS 5.2.2) ---
Defaults use_pty
Defaults requiretty
Defaults noexec
Defaults umask=0077
Defaults umask_override

# --- 3. Authentication &amp; Credential Cache Hardening (CIS 5.2.5-5.2.6 / DISA STIG) ---
Defaults authenticate
Defaults !visiblepw
Defaults !pwfeedback
Defaults passwd_timeout=1
Defaults passwd_tries=3
Defaults timestamp_timeout=5
Defaults timestamp_type=tty

# --- 4. Sudoedit Symlink &amp; Directory Traversal Protection (defense-in-depth, not ANSSI BP-028) ---
Defaults !sudoedit_follow
Defaults sudoedit_checkdir

# --- 5. Audit Logging &amp; I/O Telemetry (CIS 5.2.3 / DISA STIG) ---
Defaults logfile=&#34;/var/log/sudo.log&#34;
Defaults log_input, log_output
Defaults iolog_dir=&#34;/var/log/sudo-io/%{user}&#34;
Defaults mail_badpass, mail_no_user, mail_no_perms

# --- 6. Explicit Positive Host, User, Runas &amp; Command Aliases (ANSSI BP-028 R40/R42) ---
# Never use negation (!) in aliases or specs; define explicit positive allowlists.
Host_Alias  PROD_WEB_SERVERS = web01.internal.example, web02.internal.example
Host_Alias  PROD_DB_SERVERS  = db01.internal.example

User_Alias  WEB_OPERATORS    = %sudo_webops
User_Alias  DB_OPERATORS     = %sudo_dbops
User_Alias  SECURITY_AUDIT   = %sudo_auditors

Runas_Alias WEB_SVC_USER     = www-data
Runas_Alias DB_SVC_USER      = postgres

# --- 7. Least-Privilege Rules with Explicit Arguments &amp; SHA-256 Digest Pinning ---
# ANSSI BP-028 R40: Prefer dedicated non-root target accounts where root is unnecessary.
# ANSSI BP-028 R43: Pin exact arguments (or &#34;&#34; for zero arguments) and avoid wildcards (*).
# Note: SHA-256 digest pinning is an integrity defense-in-depth measure; it is not part of ANSSI BP-028.
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: \
    sha256:e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855 \
    /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh &#34;&#34;

DB_OPERATORS PROD_DB_SERVERS = (DB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: NOEXEC: \
    sha256:a665a45920422f9d417e4867efdc4fb8a04a1f3fff1fa07e998e86f7f7a27ae3 \
    /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/pg-ControlledCheck.sh --status

# Controlled service status inspection via dedicated root-owned wrapper (avoids GTFObins systemctl pager inheritance):
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (root) PASSWD: NOEXEC: \
    sha256:2c26b46b68ffc68ff99b453c1d30413413422d706483bfa0f98a5e886266e7ae \
    /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/nginx-service-status.sh &#34;&#34;

# Read-only diagnostic command with explicit argument constraint:
SECURITY_AUDIT ALL = (root) PASSWD: NOEXEC: \
    /usr/sbin/auditctl -s

# --- 8. Safe Configuration Editing via sudoedit (ANSSI BP-028 R44) ---
# Never grant direct sudo access to vim/nano/emacs/less; use sudoedit on explicit application files.
WEB_OPERATORS PROD_WEB_SERVERS = (WEB_SVC_USER) PASSWD: \
    sudoedit /etc/nginx/conf.d/app-vhost.conf

# --- 9. Modular Drop-In Directory ---
# Note: Only enable #includedir after verifying /etc/sudoers.d and all files inside are root:root 0440.
# #includedir /etc/sudoers.d

# --- 10. SELinux Context Elevation for Administrators (DISA STIG V-272484 / RHEL-08-010455) ---
# This baseline intentionally grants no administrator-grade rules (no ALL commands, no ALL runas).
# If such rules are required on SELinux hosts, elevate the SELinux context explicitly:
# %sudo_admins ALL=(ALL) TYPE=sysadm_t ROLE=sysadm_r ALL
</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Satisfies Rules:</strong>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-001" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-001">SUDO-DEF-001</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-002" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-002">SUDO-DEF-002</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-003" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-003">SUDO-DEF-003</button>
