            <article class="template-section-card" id="tpl-dropin-example">
                <div class="template-section-header">
                    <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                        <div class="finding-meta-top">
                            <span class="framework-req-code">Modular Drop-In</span>
                            <span class="location-pill">/etc/sudoers.d/10-app-deploy (mode 0440, owner root:root)</span>
                        </div>
                        <h3 class="finding-title">2. Hardened /etc/sudoers.d/10-app-deploy Drop-In Policy</h3>
                    </div>
                    <button type="button" class="action-btn" data-action="copy-section-pre">Copy Template</button>
                </div>
                <p class="finding-desc">Example modular drop-in file for application deployment teams. Demonstrates scoped Defaults that tighten (never weaken) global settings, explicit working directory pinning (CWD=/opt/app), and non-root target execution.</p>
                <ul class="template-highlights">
                    <li>Scoped Defaults:APP_DEPLOYERS sets timestamp_timeout=0 so every invocation prompts for a password.</li>
                    <li>Uses explicit CWD=/opt/app rather than wildcard CWD=* (preventing SUDO-CMD-009).</li>
                    <li>Executes as dedicated unprivileged account (APP_USER) rather than root (ANSSI BP-028 R40).</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="finding-remediation">
                    <div class="remediation-code-header">
                        <span class="remediation-code-label">/etc/sudoers.d/10-app-deploy (mode 0440, owner root:root)</span>
                        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                    </div>
                    <pre tabindex="0"><code># ==============================================================================
# /etc/sudoers.d/10-app-deploy — Scoped Application Deployment Policy
# Validate before activation: visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/10-app-deploy
# ==============================================================================

User_Alias  APP_DEPLOYERS = %sudo_appdeploy
Host_Alias  APP_NODES     = app01.internal.example, app02.internal.example
Runas_Alias APP_USER      = appservice

# Scoped Defaults for APP_DEPLOYERS: tighten credential timeout to 0 (always prompt)
Defaults:APP_DEPLOYERS timestamp_timeout=0, passwd_timeout=1, use_pty, noexec

# Allow running the root-owned deployment wrapper as &#39;appservice&#39; with a fixed working directory:
APP_DEPLOYERS APP_NODES = (APP_USER) CWD=/opt/app PASSWD: NOEXEC: \
    sha256:9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08 \
    /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/deploy-release.sh --env=production

# Allow safe configuration editing on a single application file via sudoedit:
APP_DEPLOYERS APP_NODES = (APP_USER) PASSWD: \
    sudoedit /etc/appservice/production.ini</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Satisfies Rules:</strong>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-DEF-015" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-DEF-015">SUDO-DEF-015</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-006" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-006">SUDO-CMD-006</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-009" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-009">SUDO-CMD-009</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-012" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-012">SUDO-CMD-012</button>
                </div>
            </article>
            <article class="template-section-card" id="tpl-wrapper-script">
                <div class="template-section-header">
                    <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                        <div class="finding-meta-top">
                            <span class="framework-req-code">GTFObins Mitigation</span>
                            <span class="location-pill">/usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh (mode 0750, owner root:root)</span>
                        </div>
                        <h3 class="finding-title">3. Hardened Root-Owned Wrapper Script Pattern (Eliminating Wildcards &amp; GTFObins)</h3>
                    </div>
                    <button type="button" class="action-btn" data-action="copy-section-pre">Copy Template</button>
                </div>
                <p class="finding-desc">When operators need to run utilities listed in GTFObins (such as find, tar, journalctl, docker, or scripts with variable parameters), wrap the logic inside a root-owned script with strict input validation, zero arguments (or an allowlisted enum), and pin its SHA-256 hash in sudoers.</p>
                <ul class="template-highlights">
                    <li>Sanitizes PATH, LC_ALL, and umask inside the script itself as defense-in-depth.</li>
                    <li>Rejects unexpected positional arguments ($# -ne 0) and uses fixed absolute paths.</li>
                    <li>Eliminates the need to grant direct sudo access to GTFObins binaries or shell wildcards (*).</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="finding-remediation">
                    <div class="remediation-code-header">
                        <span class="remediation-code-label">/usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh (mode 0750, owner root:root)</span>
                        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                    </div>
                    <pre tabindex="0"><code>#!/usr/bin/env -S bash -euo pipefail
# ==============================================================================
# /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh
# Owner: root:root | Permissions: 0750
# Purpose: Validate and reload Nginx configuration without exposing shell/GTFObins
# ==============================================================================

export PATH=&#34;/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin&#34;
export LC_ALL=&#34;C&#34;
umask 0077

if [[ &#34;$#&#34; -ne 0 ]]; then
    echo &#34;Error: This wrapper does not accept any command-line arguments.&#34; &gt;&amp;2
    exit 2
fi

# 1. Verify configuration syntax using absolute binary path
/usr/sbin/nginx -t -c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf

# 2. Reload service cleanly via systemctl
/usr/bin/systemctl reload nginx.service</code></pre>
                </div>
                <div class="req-rule-pills">
                    <strong class="req-pills-label">Satisfies Rules:</strong>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-004" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-004">SUDO-CMD-004</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-006" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-006">SUDO-CMD-006</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-011" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-011">SUDO-CMD-011</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-CMD-012" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-CMD-012">SUDO-CMD-012</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-GTFO-001" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-GTFO-001">SUDO-GTFO-001</button>
                    <button type="button" class="finding-id" data-jump-rule="SUDO-GTFO-002" title="Filter Audit Report by rule SUDO-GTFO-002">SUDO-GTFO-002</button>
                </div>
            </article>
            <article class="template-section-card" id="tpl-filesystem-perms">
                <div class="template-section-header">
                    <div class="framework-req-title-wrap">
                        <div class="finding-meta-top">
                            <span class="framework-req-code">Host Hardening &amp; Verification</span>
                            <span class="location-pill">Root Shell Remediation &amp; CI Verification Commands</span>
                        </div>
                        <h3 class="finding-title">4. Filesystem Permissions, Digest Calculation &amp; Validation Runbook</h3>
                    </div>
                    <button type="button" class="action-btn" data-action="copy-section-pre">Copy Template</button>
                </div>
                <p class="finding-desc">Copy-pasteable commands to enforce CIS 5.2.1 ownership and permissions across /etc/sudoers, /etc/sudoers.d, /usr/bin/sudo, and wrapper directories (ANSSI BP-028 R38 additionally recommends restricting sudo binary execution to a dedicated group), followed by syntax and security audit verification.</p>
                <ul class="template-highlights">
                    <li>Satisfies every system permission check (SUDO-SYS-PERM-001 through SUDO-SYS-PERM-013).</li>
                    <li>Uses visudo -c -f to prevent syntax lockouts before deploying changes.</li>
                    <li>Verifies zero policy or host findings with sudo-check.</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="finding-remediation">
                    <div class="remediation-code-header">
                        <span class="remediation-code-label">Root Shell Remediation &amp; CI Verification Commands</span>
                        <button type="button" class="copy-btn" data-action="copy-code">Copy</button>
                    </div>
                    <pre tabindex="0"><code># 1. Enforce strict ownership and 0440 permissions on /etc/sudoers (SUDO-SYS-PERM-002..005, 011)
sudo chown root:root /etc/sudoers
sudo chmod 0440 /etc/sudoers

# 2. Secure /etc/sudoers.d directory and all drop-in files (CIS 5.2.1)
sudo chown root:root /etc/sudoers.d
sudo chmod 0750 /etc/sudoers.d
sudo find /etc/sudoers.d -maxdepth 1 -type f -exec chown root:root {} &#43; -exec chmod 0440 {} &#43;

# 3. Verify /usr/bin/sudo binary ownership and SUID mode 4755 (SUDO-SYS-PERM-007..010)
sudo chown root:root /usr/bin/sudo
sudo chmod 4755 /usr/bin/sudo

# 4. Secure dedicated wrapper script directory and compute SHA-256 digest (SUDO-SYS-PERM-012..013, SUDO-CMD-012)
sudo install -d -o root -g root -m 0755 /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers
sudo chown root:root /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh
sudo chmod 0750 /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh
sudo sha256sum /usr/local/sbin/sudo-wrappers/reload-nginx-config.sh

# 5. Validate sudoers syntax with visudo and run sudo-check verification
sudo visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers
sudo sudo-check scan /etc/sudoers --fail-on=LOW</code></pre>
                </div>
